WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.816
Look at the back of the International Space Station — the Russian end.

00:00:04.816 --> 00:00:10.801
The module furthest back, where the Soyuz and Progress vehicles dock, is called Zarya .

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:13.003
In Russian, that means "sunrise.

00:00:13.003 --> 00:00:16.649
" Zarya was the first piece of the ISS ever launched.

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:18.025
November 20th, 1998.

00:00:18.025 --> 00:00:20.227
Proton-K rocket out of Baikonur.

00:00:20.227 --> 00:00:30.546
For the first two weeks, Zarya was just a single Russian-built canister in orbit, alone, providing its own power and attitude control while it waited.

00:00:30.546 --> 00:00:33.298
But Zarya isn't really a Russian module.

00:00:33.298 --> 00:00:45.063
It is technically called a Functional Cargo Block — F G B — a design built by Khrunichev, the same Moscow-based design bureau that built the Salyut and Mir space stations.

00:00:45.063 --> 00:00:47.815
And Zarya is owned by the United States.

00:00:47.815 --> 00:01:02.331
NASA paid for it — about two hundred and fifty million dollars in 1995 — because the original American design for the first ISS module ran late, and NASA needed a stand-in to keep the assembly schedule on track.

00:01:02.331 --> 00:01:12.238
Russian-built, American-owned, launched into a partnership between programs that, six years earlier, had been on opposite sides of the Cold War.

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:17.054
It is hard to overstate what that arrangement looked like at the time.

00:01:17.054 --> 00:01:25.585
Two weeks after Zarya reached orbit, Endeavour launched on STS-88 carrying Unity — Node 1 — the first American-built module.

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:33.359
On December 6th, 1998, the crew of Endeavour grabbed Zarya with the Shuttle's robotic arm and joined it to Unity.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:42.028
That moment — Zarya meeting Unity — is when the International Space Station went from "two separate satellites" to "a station.

00:01:42.028 --> 00:01:49.939
" Over the next twelve and a half years, twenty more pressurized modules joined them, one Shuttle flight at a time.

00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:57.576
All of it built outward, in space, from the original Russian-American handshake at the back end of the station.

